<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Murcia</h1><p class="st-meta">1011 CE – 1091 CE (approx.) · capital Murcia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A south-eastern taifa that shifted between local rulers and powerful neighbours before falling to the Almoravids, with Murcia's huerta as its core.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>, heir to the old Visigothic-era region of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tudmir">Tudmir</a>, broke from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> around 1011. For its first decades it was usually governed for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amirids">Amirid</a> rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, until the local family of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tahirid-sultanate">Banu Tahir</a> took effective control under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ahmad-ibn-tahir">Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Tahir</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segura-river">Segura</a> valley's irrigation supported grain, fruit and textile production. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tahirid-sultanate">Banu Tahir</a> ruled as patricians, nominally under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>. In 1078 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutamid-ibn-abbad">al-Mutamid</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a> sent his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-ammar">Ibn Ammar</a>, to seize the city; Ibn Ammar and then his officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-rashiq">Ibn Rashiq</a> ruled it in defiance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-rashiq">Ibn Rashiq's</a> double dealing with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, which held the nearby fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aledo">Aledo</a>, made him unpopular. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a> in 1091. It re-emerged as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-taifa-of-murcia">Second Taifa of Murcia</a> in 1147.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-murcia.jpg" alt="Taifa of Murcia" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Murcia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1011 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Murcia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Tahir, Ibn Ammar, Ibn Rashiq</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Mozarabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Murcia (taifa)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1051&amp;focus=taifa-of-murcia" aria-label="Taifa of Murcia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4652 812 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Murcia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4972 1068L4938 1106M4972 1068L4850 1088M4972 1068L4799 1289"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4938 1106h0M4850 1088h0M4799 1289h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4972 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4972 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Murcia. <a href="/atlas?year=1051&amp;focus=taifa-of-murcia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

